            "data": {
                "label": "Purdue Model (Simple)",
                "sequence": "0",
                "user_properties": [
                    [
                        "rdfs:comment",
                        "The Purdue Enterprise Reference Architecture (PERA), or \"Purdue Model\", is a reference architecture for enterprise control.  \nIt was developed by an industry consortium led by Purdue University and outlines several hierarchical levels of control starting\nat the physical process (level 0) and rising up to the enterprise level (level 5).  Levels 0-3 represent the core operational \ntechnology domain (OT) parts of the architecture, whereas levels 4-5 are general purpose information technology (IT). The \nscope of time and space increase as the levels increase, with the nearest and most time-critical functions occurring at lower levels.\n\nSince its initial definition and standardization in ISA-95, it has been interpreted and extended many times, but the basic \nhierarchical model is useful as an organizing principle. Here we include two frequently recommended DMZ layers.\n\nIn some cases, the original level names have been updated and the Purdue Model names are in brackets.\n\n\n\n"
                    ]
                ]
            },
